index 5342204..afcb28e 100644
--- a/specs/state.md
+++ b/specs/state.md
@@ -245,6 +245,40 @@ Checksum and program-identity compatibility:
 - Equality of step, checkpoint, call-frame, or other persisted identities is not by itself evidence that a run can resume across changed source. The root workflow checksum remains an independent compatibility guard.
 - Any future cross-source compatibility mechanism must be a tested atomic upgrader that owns both checksum and program-identity compatibility, validates the complete old-to-new transition, and either commits the compatible state as one operation or leaves the old state unchanged. Evidence records, identity deltas, aliases, or partial remaps are not such an upgrader.
 
+## Checksum-Compatible Resume Projection Integrity
+
+A run whose root workflow checksum still matches can nonetheless carry persisted identities the current program no longer owns (a stale or renamed step id, an impossible loop iteration, a call frame whose caller or import alias changed, two resumable frames for one call). The state schema stays `"2.1"`; pre-v2.0 state and pre-2.1 reusable-call state remain rejected. Resume audits each scope it will execute against the projection of the bundle that owns that scope and stops before any mutation or effect when the audit fails.
+
+Audit scope and ordering:
+- After schema and root-checksum acceptance, every ordinary root resume audits the persisted root scope against the entry workflow's current projection. `orchestrate resume` does so before observability overrides, executor-session or process metadata, executor construction, and the execution prologue; on failure it exits 1, prints the reason to standard error, and persists only the failure envelope below. The initial root-checksum precheck keeps its behavior (exit 1, nothing written).
+- A `WorkflowExecutor` resuming as the structural root (not a child call-frame executor) recomputes the authoritative checksum through `StateManager.calculate_checksum`, then audits the root scope, immediately before its prologue. A child call-frame executor does not repeat the root guard.
+- A reached call (a call step executing during resume) resolves its caller identity with one `WorkflowStateProjection.resolve_call_boundary(slot_index, call_step_id)` lookup: exactly one current boundary must own it, and that boundary's current import alias selects the callee bundle (the persisted alias is checked and never redirects selection). Frame shape, caller boundary, alias, and retry lineage are revalidated after the parent call visit/start publication and after the call's authored-input, managed/runtime-input, write-root, checksum, and resume-bound-input validation, but before the callee's frame or state is created or changed and before any callee effect. Nested scopes apply the same rule recursively.
+- The auditor reads only the bundle and state it is given. It loads no workflow or bundle, computes no checksum, reads no file, mutates nothing, reads no procedure-migration or identity-retirement evidence, and keeps no persisted audit state.
+
+Identity rules:
+- Every present explicit durable identity (`steps.<name>.step_id`, `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.<id>.call_step_id`) must resolve to exactly one candidate in its owning scope. Qualified loop and call identities resolve only through projection-owned candidates (`enumerate_resume_slots`); resume never splits, normalizes, prefix-matches, reconstructs, remaps, or backfills ids and never selects by workflow, module, procedure name, basename, path suffix, or persisted alias.
+- An absent `steps.<name>.step_id` stays compatible for every schema-valid row the presentation/name/order fallback supports, whatever its status; resume leaves the field absent. `current_step.step_id`, `call_frames.*.call_step_id`, call-frame current position, and loop progress selectors remain mandatory.
+- `for_each` bookkeeping: indices are unique non-boolean non-negative integers within `items`; `current_index` is in range and not completed. `repeat_until` admits exactly four forms: active (non-negative `current_iteration` not in `completed_iterations`, consistent condition fields); terminal condition success (`current_iteration: null`, `last_condition_result: true`, not exhausted); terminal successful exhaustion (covers the declared maximum range, `last_condition_result: false`, `exhausted: true`, completed loop step); terminal failed exhaustion (same range, `exhausted` absent or false, failed loop step with `error.type: repeat_until_iterations_exhausted`). Valid bookkeeping does not excuse a stale or out-of-scope loop-local id.
+
+Call frames and Workflow Lisp retry lineage:
+- Every frame validates caller identity, import alias, boundary ownership, and status (`running`, `failed`, `completed`). Completed frames are unlimited historical records and never resumable. A boundary whose callee is not Workflow Lisp has at most one non-completed frame.
+- A Workflow Lisp boundary is recognised only by the typed capability `provenance.frontend_kind == "workflow_lisp"`. Its non-completed frames form one lineage `B`, `B::retry::<n>` with at most one running member and any number of failed predecessors. Mixed bases, several running members, duplicate ordinals, malformed or nested retry markers, unknown statuses, and a caller or alias differing from the boundary's fail closed. The next fresh retry id is the lowest unused positive ordinal over all members.
+- With a running member: its checksum and resume-bound-input validation run first and win on failure; then each failed predecessor is checksum-validated and audited in ordinal order; then the running member's scope is audited and resumed. Without one, every failed predecessor is validated and audited before the fresh retry frame is allocated.
+- A completed frame whose id collides with the frame a resumed call would occupy is rejected before lineage grouping with the invalid-resume-state contract violation (`reason: call_resume_state_invalid`, `detail: completed_call_frame_id_collision`); no child state manager or executor is created.
+
+Failure envelopes:
+- A structural-root checksum recheck failure persists `error` = `{"type": "workflow_checksum_mismatch", "message": "Workflow has been modified since the run started", "context": {"workflow_file", "persisted_checksum", "current_checksum", "reason"}}` with `reason` one of `workflow_modified`, `missing_recorded_checksum`, `missing_workflow_path`, `workflow_unavailable`. A root projection failure persists the diagnostic below. Both change only the root `status` (to `failed`), `error`, and `updated_at`; already-open session/process metadata may remain and a failing session close cannot replace the diagnostic.
+- A reached-callee failure before the callee is constructed becomes the caller's failed call-step result and the scope/run error; the callee frame and state stay untouched, ancestor frames persist ordinary failed snapshots, and the exact same diagnostic is promoted unchanged to the root (never wrapped in `call_failed`).
+- `resume_projection_integrity_error` is sticky and non-routable: authored `success`/`failure`/`always` routes, `on_error: continue`, and `for_each`/`repeat_until` continuation cannot consume it. Configured finalization may run, but a finalization failure is supplemental: `finalization.status: failed` with `failure` = `{"step", "step_id", "error"}` where `error` is the cleanup's projection error or null for an ordinary failure; it never wraps, replaces, clears, or downgrades the run diagnostic. When the projection error arises only inside finalization it becomes the run error and `finalization.failure.error`.
+- An unchanged `current_step` left by a projection or checksum envelope is forensic: status snapshots show the step as `pending` (not `running`, not `in progress`), and heartbeat and stalled-run interpretation do not treat it as live.
+
+Diagnostic (stable, privacy-preserving):
+- `{"type": "resume_projection_integrity_error", "message": "Resume projection integrity audit failed: <reason>", "context": {...}}` with always-present context keys `diagnostic_schema` (`"resume_projection_integrity_error.v1"`), `reason`, `scope_path` (`{"kind": "root", "workflow_file"}` then one `{"kind": "call_frame", "frame_id"}` per frame entered), `field`, `offending_value` (scalar or null), `expected_owner` (`workflow_file`, `workflow_checksum`, `projection_scope`), `candidate_count`, and `call_boundary_step_id`.
+- Reasons: `unknown_explicit_step_id`, `presentation_slot_mismatch`, `out_of_scope_step_id`, `unclaimed_explicit_step_row`, `missing_required_identity`, `missing_call_boundary`, `ambiguous_call_boundary`, `ambiguous_resumable_call_frame`, `persisted_import_alias_mismatch`, `missing_imported_bundle`, `unsupported_shape`, `invalid_loop_progress`.
+- Diagnostics never expose bound inputs, context values, artifacts, prompts, provider output, secrets, whole frame state or projections, or the contents of a malformed container used as an identity.
+
+Classic-YAML import aliases are validated before bundle construction: an alias occurring more than once among the effective `imports` entries (duplicate keys, a `<<` merge inside `imports`, repeated top-level `imports` sections, or merge sources that each carry `imports`) is reported once as `imports.<alias>: duplicate import alias` naming each `line L, column C`; repeated top-level `imports` sections with distinct aliases are reported once as `imports: duplicate top-level mapping`.
+
 Workflow Lisp lexical-checkpoint default resume:
 - Node-local restore selection is primary. A prior-boundary fallback is allowed only when the restart node owns lexical checkpoint metadata and restore selection positively reports typed `record_absent` for that node's next boundary.
 - Only the canonical checkpoint index whose `program_point_id` matches the runtime-plan point and whose `storage_allocation_id` matches the canonical lexical-checkpoint-index allocation may establish absence. A missing canonical index or a valid canonical index with an empty `records` list is `record_absent`; a present unreadable, malformed, incomplete, foreign, stale, or otherwise invalid index is `record_present_unusable` and fails closed with a stable diagnostic.
